Corporate patches:

Probably be a rather contravesrial thread, but I thought it might
provoke some interesting responses.

While the nathack community has lots of excellent coders working on
patches and branches, I'm always frustrated by the fact that there are
never enough to code all the cool ideas that *I* want to see in the
game. (Yes, I know I should shut up and write the patches myself, but
it would probably take several years of education and practise that I
don't have time for to reach the required competency in C).

The problem is that the non-commercial nature of nethack- which is one
of it's great strengths, imo- unfortunately means that ppl can only
ever work on it in their spare time. People don't get a lot of spare
time, and very often they don't want to spend it writing code,
especially if they write code all day long for their job anyway. I
don't want to seem ungrateful for all the fantastic work put in by
those who have contributed to the game over the years, but what if
there were a way to harness the productivity of ppl coding for a
living, while retaining the free, open-source nature of the game? To
draw businesses into the community as voluntary contributors on an
equal standing to the individuals who make it up at present?

While Nethack's license does (I believe) permit the sale of
nethack-derived material, as long as certain conditions are met, I'm
not suggesting that business entities produce commercial versions of
nethack. I don't imagine this being very successful, for one thing, and
I do think it would take something away from the game and the
community. What I propose instead is that companies produce free,
open-source patches and release them to the public, just as an
individual member of the community might do.

Why would they do this? Since when have businesses given anything away
for free? Well actually, they give away free stuff all the time. Never
had a free sample of a product? Never seen a photo of a grinning
marketing manager handing over an oversized cheque to a local
children's hospital or charity fundraiser? It's called promotion.
Advertising. Public relations.

Businesses have really picked up on online advertising in the last few
years. Not just banners ads, which are merely an extension of
traditional poster, print and TV advertising, but more novel concepts
like memetic advertising. I wonder if companies would be interested in
using software like nethack to advertise. It would effectively be a
code-based, rather than mpeg or flash-game based form of memetic
advertising.

Let's take nethack's much-YANIed "photography patch". While there is at
least one working photographry patch, it would suit my example better
if there wasn't. Suppose there was no photography patch- why couldn't a
company like (for example) Nikon write one? A technology firm like that
ought to have lots of competent IT people kicking around that would be
capable of undertaking such a task. They could rename the "expensive
camera" to "a Nikon camera" and write the patch. Release it to the
public and hey look- instant geek-cred. We get our patch, they get an
image-boost, everyone's happy.

Of course the first worry would be that the dungeons of doom would
become some kind of hideous, billboard-packed advertising arcade. I
think this is unlikely. The patches produced by advertisers would be
entirely optional- if ppl didn't like them , they'd simply go back to
playing without the patch. Blatant and intrusive advertising would
annoy and alienate users, which would be counter-productive. They'd
have to make their message subtle, amusing and clever (the hallmarks of
a decent nethack feature.)

What's more, the patch would (ideally) be open-source so it would be
relatively simple for someone to alter the code and remove the bits
they didn't like. In cases where the advertising was too extreme, this
kind of action might be reasonable, but for the most part I think we'd
be happy to give credit where it's due, as long as the advertising
wasn't too intrusive.  Of course, under the terms of the license, the
modifier would have to acknowledge the original author of the patch (ie
the advertiser) in their docs, so as long as the code was worthwhile,
the advertiser's message would live on.

Normally I'm very cynical about the business world, and their ability
to ruin good things completely with single-minded profit-vision, but I
think in theory this might just work. I mean, they'd only be producing
patches, which are entirely optional anyway, and it would be nice to
try to teach some of these companies the value of NOT owning something.


Potential problems:
1- Product twinking. Manufacturer X adds a cool new item to the game.
Manufaturer Yhas a similar idea, but they have to make their item more
powerful and cool than manufacturer X's item. Cue massive uber-item
arms-race that chucks play balance out the window.

2- How to get the message to the right people? Anyone here work for an
eccentric, progressive advertising company?

3- Finding appropriate companies. This proposal would be a lot easier
if modern cutting-edge companies dealt in axes, body armour and magical
accoutrements rather than mobile phones, engine management systems and
insurance policies. However, I'm sure there are numerous possibilities,
especially in branches like slashem, which includes a lot more
out-of-genre junk than nethack. Of course, they don't necessarily have
to produce patches that feature their own products, but
product-placement seems the most obvious appoach.

4- Companies trying to take ownership of nethack. Given that nethack
has a proper open-source license, this is unlikely, but if they got all
protective about their code and started suing people things could get
really ugly.

So... who's first with the comments/ suggestions/ flames/ hysterical
rants?



